EXT. ROADSIDE FRUIT STAND - DAY

A plywood shed on a gravel shoulder. Crates of peaches. A hand-painted sign: HONESTY BOX - $3 A BAG. Heat shimmers off the highway.

GRACIELA HUNT, 50s, in a straw hat, arranges peaches in a pyramid. Her nephew BASTIAN, 20s, sits on an upturned crate, eating a peach and watching the road. A dusty pickup rolls up and stops.

A MAN in a cowboy hat steps out. This is OTIS WREN, 40s. He nods at them, picks up a bag, and counts out three singles. He drops them into the tin box.

OTIS
Thank you kindly.

GRACIELA
Peaches are good this year.

OTIS
They look it.

Bastian stops chewing. Graciela straightens a peach that did not need straightening. Otis fishes a fourth dollar from his wallet and lays it beside the tin.

OTIS (CONT'D)
For the bag.

GRACIELA
Bags are free.

OTIS
Then for the view.

He climbs into the pickup. The engine turns. Graciela watches the tailgate recede until the road shimmers it away. Then she opens the tin, counts the bills, and counts them again.

BASTIAN
It's all there?

GRACIELA
It's four dollars.

BASTIAN
It's three.

GRACIELA
It's four.

She slides the extra dollar into her apron. Bastian bites the peach.
